<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Acalan</h1><p class="st-meta">1350 CE – 1557 CE (approx.) · capital Itzamkanac</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Chontal Maya trading kingdom on the Candelaria river in Campeche, where the last Aztec ruler Cuauhtemoc was executed in 1525.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Acalan, meaning 'place of canoes' in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>, was the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chontal-maya">Chontal Maya</a> (Mactun) along the Candelaria river south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laguna-de-terminos">Laguna de Términos</a>. Its dynasty, recorded in the Chontal text of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paxbolon-maldonado-papers">Paxbolon-Maldonado papers</a>, traced its origins to the 14th century, when its rulers settled at Itzamkanac.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Acalan was a merchant state whose canoes carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a>, cloth, salt and other goods along the Gulf coast between central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán</a>, and its ruler was said to be the wealthiest merchant of the land. Itzamkanac was a large town with many temples, and the kingdom controlled scattered dependent settlements.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1525 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> passed through on his march to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Honduras</a> and executed Cuauhtemoc there. Acalan submitted to Spanish rule under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paxbolonacha">Paxbolonacha</a>, and in 1557 the population was moved to Tixchel on the coast; Itzamkanac was later abandoned.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1350 CE – 1557 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.57%;width:4.12%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1350 CE – 1557 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Itzamkanac</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Paxbolonacha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chontal Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Acalan-Tixchel, Itzamkanac</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1454&amp;focus=acalan" aria-label="Acalan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2217 1505 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Acalan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2537 1761L4910 989M2537 1761L2590 1658"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M2590 1658h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2537 1761h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2537 1761h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Itzamkanac. <a href="/atlas?year=1454&amp;focus=acalan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
